course: dictionary
lesson: 3420
title: Extended: longer, bigger, or stretched out
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Extended is the past form of the verb extend, so it can mean made longer or made bigger. Context tells you which meaning fits. «The shop hours were extended for the holidays» means the shop stayed open for longer. Ask yourself: has something been made longer or bigger than before?
explain: Sometimes extended means stretched out to full length, as in «with arms extended». You can picture someone holding their arms straight out. Use the picture the words make to check your meaning.
explain: An extended family includes grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins, not just parents and children. In «In Auckland, Mihi's whānau (Māori for extended family) is huge», the family is large because it includes many relatives.
fact: Extended means made longer in time or length, or made bigger.
fact: «An extended holiday» is a holiday that lasts longer than usual.
fact: «With arms extended» means with arms stretched out to full length.
fact: «The Ming rebuilt and greatly extended walls» means they made the walls much longer and bigger.
fact: An extended family is a family that includes grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins. Whānau is the Māori word for it.
exercise: «The shop hours were extended for the holidays.» What does extended mean here? | Folded up | Painted | Made shorter | *Made longer
exercise: «She stood with arms extended to catch the ball.» What does extended mean here? | *Stretched out to full length | Hidden behind her back | Tucked into her pockets | Pressed tightly together
exercise: What is an extended family? | A family that lives in a very big house | *A family that includes grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins | A family that has just moved to a new country | Only a mum, a dad and their children
test: «The Ming greatly extended the walls.» What does extended mean here? | *Made longer and bigger | Moved to the sea | Knocked down | Painted white
test: Which word is the past form of extend? | Extending | Extra | Extensive | *Extended
exam: «The teacher extended the deadline for our homework.» What does extended mean here? | Printed twice | Made earlier, giving us less time | *Made later, giving us more time | Cancelled completely
